### What happens when you convert List to Set?

The Set constructor iterates the List and adds each element. Since Sets reject duplicates, only the first occurrence of each element is kept.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class ListToSetDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> accounts = List.of(
            "SAV2001", "CUR3001", "SAV2001", "FD4001", "CUR3001", "SAV2002"
        );
        System.out.println("Original list: " + accounts);
        System.out.println("List size: " + accounts.size()); // 6

        // HashSet: removes duplicates, loses order
        Set<String> hashSet = new HashSet<>(accounts);
        System.out.println("\nHashSet:       " + hashSet);
        System.out.println("Size: " + hashSet.size()); // 4

        // LinkedHashSet: removes duplicates, keeps insertion order
        Set<String> linkedSet = new LinkedHashSet<>(accounts);
        System.out.println("\nLinkedHashSet: " + linkedSet);
        System.out.println("Size: " + linkedSet.size()); // 4

        // TreeSet: removes duplicates, sorts
        Set<String> treeSet = new TreeSet<>(accounts);
        System.out.println("\nTreeSet:       " + treeSet);
        System.out.println("Size: " + treeSet.size()); // 4
    }
}
```

```text
Original list: [SAV2001, CUR3001, SAV2001, FD4001, CUR3001, SAV2002]
List size: 6

HashSet:       [FD4001, SAV2002, CUR3001, SAV2001]
Size: 4

LinkedHashSet: [SAV2001, CUR3001, FD4001, SAV2002]
Size: 4

TreeSet:       [CUR3001, FD4001, SAV2001, SAV2002]
Size: 4
```

### How do you convert back from Set to List?

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class SetToListDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Set<String> accountSet = new LinkedHashSet<>(List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001", "FD4001"));

        // Method 1: ArrayList constructor
        List<String> list1 = new ArrayList<>(accountSet);

        // Method 2: List.copyOf (Java 10+, immutable)
        List<String> list2 = List.copyOf(accountSet);

        // Method 3: Stream
        List<String> list3 = accountSet.stream().toList();

        System.out.println("List: " + list1);
    }
}
```

```text
List: [SAV2001, CUR3001, FD4001]
```

### How do you check how many duplicates were removed?

```java
List<String> original = List.of("A", "B", "A", "C", "B", "A");
Set<String> unique = new LinkedHashSet<>(original);
int duplicateCount = original.size() - unique.size();
System.out.println("Duplicates removed: " + duplicateCount); // 3
```

### What about custom objects?

When converting a list of custom objects to a set, the Set uses `equals()` and `hashCode()` to determine uniqueness. Without proper overrides, each object instance is treated as unique.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;

public class CustomObjectDedup {
    record Account(String id, String holder, BigDecimal balance) {}
    // Records auto-generate equals/hashCode based on all components

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Account> accounts = List.of(
            new Account("SAV2001", "Rajesh", new BigDecimal("50000")),
            new Account("CUR3001", "Priya", new BigDecimal("75000")),
            new Account("SAV2001", "Rajesh", new BigDecimal("50000")), // duplicate
            new Account("SAV2001", "Rajesh", new BigDecimal("60000"))  // different balance!
        );

        Set<Account> unique = new LinkedHashSet<>(accounts);
        System.out.println("Unique accounts: " + unique.size()); // 3
        // The last entry has different balance → different equals → not a duplicate
        unique.forEach(a -> System.out.println("  " + a));
    }
}
```

```text
Unique accounts: 3
  Account[id=SAV2001, holder=Rajesh, balance=50000]
  Account[id=CUR3001, holder=Priya, balance=75000]
  Account[id=SAV2001, holder=Rajesh, balance=60000]
```

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake 1: Using HashSet and expecting insertion order in the output

```java
// WRONG: order lost
Set<String> set = new HashSet<>(list);
for (String s : set) { System.out.print(s + " "); } // unpredictable order

// CORRECT: use LinkedHashSet for order
Set<String> set = new LinkedHashSet<>(list);
```

#### Mistake 2: Deduplicating objects without proper equals/hashCode

```java
// WRONG: class without equals/hashCode
class Account { String id; }
List<Account> list = List.of(new Account("SAV2001"), new Account("SAV2001"));
Set<Account> set = new HashSet<>(list);
// set.size() = 2! Both treated as different because default equals is identity.

// CORRECT: use record or override equals/hashCode
record Account(String id) {}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Set.copyOf(list) (Java 10+).** Creates an immutable set from the list, removing duplicates. Null elements throw NullPointerException. Iteration order is not guaranteed.

- **Collectors.toUnmodifiableSet().** In streams: `list.stream().collect(Collectors.toUnmodifiableSet())` creates an immutable set. Null and duplicates throw exceptions.

- **TreeSet conversion requires Comparable.** `new TreeSet<>(list)` requires elements to implement Comparable. Otherwise, provide a Comparator: `new TreeSet<>(comparator)` then `addAll(list)`.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you remove duplicates from a List?" – new ArrayList<>(new LinkedHashSet<>(list)).
- "What determines if two elements are duplicates?" – equals() and hashCode() for Hash/LinkedHash sets; compareTo() for TreeSet.
- "Is the size of the Set always less than the List?" – Less than or equal. Equal when there are no duplicates.
